    "narrative": "The Austin Waves lineup for today features two standout options anchored by favorable matchups and high-upside environments. **Andy Pages** (69.36) remains the premier play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Jack Kochanowicz, whose high xERA (5.12) creates a high floor for production. While the presence of an elite defensive catcher and a high hold rate for stolen bases limits his upside slightly, his overall profile is bolstered by a strong order position. Similarly, **Sam Antonacci** (67.67) is a must-start at the top of the lineup. Facing a mid-tier arm in Painter, **Sam Antonacci** capitalizes on a 0.379 xwOBA and favorable winds blowing out, maximizing his potential for runs in the leadoff spot. **Gunnar Henderson** (63.31) and **Miguel Vargas** (61.96) round out our top-tier tier; notably, **Gunnar Henderson** remains a vital engine for our roster due to his aggressive base-running against Fisher, whose slow delivery (85% SB allowance) offers an elite opportunity to secure the category in which we are most aggressively chasing efficiency.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Brayan Rocchio** (41.35), who sat right on the edge of the marginal start threshold. While **Brayan Rocchio** presented a decent base projection, his deployment in the #9 hole proved to be the disqualifying factor, as the opportunity cost of batting at the bottom of the order lowered his run-scoring probability relative to the alternatives. Meanwhile, **Coby Mayo** (38.13) and **Miguel Andujar** (34.55) fell below our 40-point efficiency floor due to prohibitive matchups against Fisher and Nolan McLean, respectively. We continue to manage our game caps by avoiding these low-probability starts, even as we monitor the health of the roster\u2014specifically the absence of **Jordan Westburg**, whose IL status necessitates constant optimization of our middle-infield depth.\n\nManagers should exercise caution regarding the volatility inherent in today's matchups. Despite favorable scores for **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (55.19) and **Jackson Merrill** (54.33), both face SP_xERA figures near or below 3.60, indicating that their production may be limited by elite pitching rather than park factors. **Dominic Smith** (42.39) is our most precarious start; his score is buoyed by a platoon advantage against Ashcraft, but he occupies a low-upside #5 spot against a pitcher who remains stingy. We will monitor weather reports for shifts in wind speed that could further complicate the outlook for **Miguel Vargas** and **Sam Antonacci**, but for now, the lineup remains optimized to prioritize stolen base potential and high-order batting positions.",
